1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,975
A person opens an application on a phone, verifies their identity, and seconds later, their documents appear.

2
00:00:05,975 --> 00:00:10,599
But the same action also creates data: who verified their identity, when, and for which procedure.

3
00:00:11,799 --> 00:00:14,357
So where does democracy lie in a system like this?

4
00:00:14,357 --> 00:00:15,852
Is it simply that everyone can use it?

5
00:00:15,852 --> 00:00:17,709
Is it that everyone can submit feedback?

6
00:00:17,709 --> 00:00:22,985
Or is it that the people affected can genuinely participate and change how the system is designed and governed?

7
00:00:24,515 --> 00:00:26,078
Greetings, lecturer and everyone.

8
00:00:26,078 --> 00:00:31,476
We are Group 14, presenting on the democratization of technology design and governance according to Andrew Feenberg.

9
00:00:31,476 --> 00:00:35,731
First, we define two foundational concepts: democracy and democratization.

10
00:00:40,931 --> 00:00:45,908
The word “democracy” comes from the ancient Greek: “demos” means the people, and “kratos” means rule.

11
00:00:45,908 --> 00:00:49,710
Its original meaning was rule by the people; later, it came to mean power belonging to the people.

12
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:56,915
In the Marxist–Leninist approach presented in the textbook on scientific socialism, democracy is a social value reflecting fundamental human rights,

13
00:00:56,915 --> 00:00:58,213
and it is understood in three dimensions.

14
00:00:58,543 --> 00:01:03,267
In terms of power, democracy means power belongs to the people, who are the masters of the state.

15
00:01:03,267 --> 00:01:08,232
In terms of social order and politics, democracy is a form, or a type, of state.

16
00:01:08,232 --> 00:01:11,467
In organizing and managing society, democracy is a principle.

17
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:14,948
Democracy is also historical; it develops together with the history of human society.

18
00:01:16,148 --> 00:01:22,505
In The State and Revolution, Lenin wrote that a democratic order is a form of state, one of the forms of the state.

19
00:01:22,505 --> 00:01:26,827
On the other hand, a democratic order means the formal recognition of equality among citizens,

20
00:01:26,827 --> 00:01:30,582
recognizing that everyone has an equal right to determine the structure of the state and to manage the state.

21
00:01:30,912 --> 00:01:36,416
What our group retains from this definition is that democracy is tied to citizens’ equal rights to organize and exercise public power.

22
00:01:37,616 --> 00:01:41,193
In Vietnam, President Ho Chi Minh wrote: “Our country is a democratic country.

23
00:01:41,193 --> 00:01:42,910
All benefits are for the people.

24
00:01:42,910 --> 00:01:44,884
All power belongs to the people.”

25
00:01:44,884 --> 00:01:52,454
The 2013 Constitution affirms that all state power belongs to the People; citizens have the right to participate in state and social management, to discuss and petition;

26
00:01:52,454 --> 00:01:56,876
the State must be open and transparent in receiving and responding to citizens’ opinions and petitions.

27
00:01:58,076 --> 00:02:01,201
Drawing on these sources, our group uses a working definition with three layers.

28
00:02:01,531 --> 00:02:06,416
The subject layer: the right to decide common matters belongs to the people, especially those affected.

29
00:02:06,416 --> 00:02:13,937
The institutional layer: this right must become a right to know, participation procedures, representative bodies, oversight, accountability, and checks on power.

30
00:02:13,937 --> 00:02:18,183
The value layer: equality, dignity, protection of human rights, and the capacity for criticism.

31
00:02:19,383 --> 00:02:22,501
We should be clear: democracy is historical and concrete.

32
00:02:22,501 --> 00:02:26,426
Each country realizes democracy through its history, institutions, and system of values.

33
00:02:26,426 --> 00:02:29,137
But that does not mean every place may define it however it wishes.

34
00:02:29,137 --> 00:02:36,718
Everywhere, democracy must answer testable questions: can people know, discuss, decide, supervise, and be protected?

35
00:02:37,048 --> 00:02:41,809
Therefore, democracy is not only majority voting; and a feedback form alone is not yet democracy.

36
00:02:47,009 --> 00:02:53,506
If democracy is a value and a form of organizing power, democratization is a process: within a specific structure,

37
00:02:53,506 --> 00:03:00,608
it expands five elements for those affected: the right to know, effective participation, the right to criticize, the right to demand accountability, and the ability to influence decisions.

38
00:03:01,808 --> 00:03:08,157
Vietnam’s 2022 Law on Grassroots Democracy also separates democracy into several levels: matters that must be made public so people know;

39
00:03:08,157 --> 00:03:15,056
matters the people discuss and decide; matters on which they give opinions before a competent authority decides; and matters of inspection and supervision.

40
00:03:15,386 --> 00:03:19,372
These levels show that knowing differs from discussing, and giving an opinion differs from deciding.

41
00:03:20,572 --> 00:03:25,072
Therefore, democratization does not mean that everyone can use an application or send an opinion.

42
00:03:25,072 --> 00:03:28,670
Moving a paper procedure onto a screen is digitization, not yet democratization.

43
00:03:28,670 --> 00:03:33,142
Democratization is not replacing expertise with a vote, nor does it mean making all personal data public.

44
00:03:34,342 --> 00:03:42,773
Participation is effective only when conditions are present: enough information to understand; access for vulnerable groups; the freedom to speak without fear of retaliation; reasoned responses;

45
00:03:42,773 --> 00:03:48,212
a path of appeal; someone responsible; and the ability to change rules or designs when evidence shows an unjust impact.

46
00:03:49,412 --> 00:03:52,528
To clarify levels of participation, our group uses a matrix.

47
00:03:52,528 --> 00:03:58,241
The rows are rights: to know, to be consulted, to decide together, and to supervise and request changes.

48
00:03:58,241 --> 00:04:01,377
The columns are levels: absent, formal, or effective.

49
00:04:01,707 --> 00:04:05,514
A system may be effective for one right but merely formal for another.

50
00:04:05,514 --> 00:04:11,889
For example, consultation with a response is the highest level of the right to be consulted, but it is still not co-decision: these are different rights.

51
00:04:13,089 --> 00:04:16,649
We should also be clear: a design change does not by itself prove democracy.

52
00:04:16,649 --> 00:04:21,902
A change may come only from a manager’s preference, from the group with the strongest voice, or may even harm vulnerable groups.

53
00:04:22,232 --> 00:04:29,556
So we must ask four questions: whose experience did the change come from; what process did it pass through; whose interests does it serve; and who is accountable for it?

54
00:04:34,756 --> 00:04:37,429
So why does philosophy ask about democracy in technology?

55
00:04:37,759 --> 00:04:44,531
Because philosophy always asks: who is the subject; how is power legitimized; which values deserve protection; and who is responsible?

56
00:04:44,531 --> 00:04:47,996
Democracy turns these questions into rights, institutions, and concrete procedures.

57
00:04:49,196 --> 00:04:54,669
The bridge from the definition of democracy to technology does not lie in similar wording, but in a fact: today,

58
00:04:54,669 --> 00:04:57,072
public power is exercised partly through technical systems.

59
00:04:57,402 --> 00:05:04,511
When a state agency verifies identity, issues documents, assesses eligibility for policies, or processes citizens’ data through software, a required data field,

60
00:05:04,511 --> 00:05:11,345
a classification criterion, or an access level determines who is served, who is refused, who can view data, and who can appeal.

61
00:05:11,345 --> 00:05:13,962
Those technical rules operate like a management decision.

62
00:05:15,162 --> 00:05:21,571
Feenberg also opened a 1992 essay with the observation that technology is one of the main sources of public power in modern society.

63
00:05:21,901 --> 00:05:26,788
From this, our group argues: if democracy is citizens’ equal right to manage the state,

64
00:05:26,788 --> 00:05:30,278
then that right must reach the technical systems through which public power is exercised.

65
00:05:31,478 --> 00:05:33,309
Here we need to define three concepts.

66
00:05:33,309 --> 00:05:41,405
Technology is not only equipment; it is a sociotechnical system of machines, software, data, standards, procedures, people, and institutions.

67
00:05:41,405 --> 00:05:47,643
Technology design is the choice of structures, functions, data, standards, and access rights, not merely interface decoration.

68
00:05:47,643 --> 00:05:54,559
Technology governance is the allocation of decision-making power, responsibility, oversight, evaluation, revision, and appeal throughout a system’s life cycle.

69
00:05:54,559 --> 00:06:01,840
And technological values are priorities such as efficiency, safety, fairness, privacy, or dignity, made concrete through technical and governance choices.

70
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:06,052
From here, democracy and technology meet in two directions.

71
00:06:06,052 --> 00:06:11,975
The first direction is using technology to practice democracy in social governance, helping people access information, submit petitions,

72
00:06:11,975 --> 00:06:13,557
supervise, and appeal more conveniently.

73
00:06:13,887 --> 00:06:19,690
The second direction is democratizing technology itself: those affected participate in designing and governing these systems.

74
00:06:20,020 --> 00:06:26,132
The two directions are connected: a digital platform truly serves democracy only when its users have a voice in how it is created and operated.

75
00:06:27,332 --> 00:06:34,423
This leads to a crucial distinction: a person may use a system every day without having any say in designing or governing it.

76
00:06:34,423 --> 00:06:36,618
This is where Andrew Feenberg enters.

77
00:06:41,818 --> 00:06:48,962
Andrew Feenberg is an American philosopher of technology and Professor Emeritus at Simon Fraser University in Canada, associated with critical theory of technology.

78
00:06:49,942 --> 00:06:55,785
Feenberg organizes views of technology along two axes: neutral or value-laden; autonomous or controlled by human beings.

79
00:06:55,785 --> 00:07:01,100
Instrumentalism sees technology as a neutral means; determinism sees technology as self-directing and shaping society.

80
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:05,435
Critical theory holds that technology is value-laden and can also be guided by human beings.

81
00:07:05,435 --> 00:07:12,496
For him, the problem is that we do not yet have suitable institutions for controlling technology, and technology can be placed in a more democratic process of design and development.

82
00:07:13,696 --> 00:07:16,264
The first point is the relative indeterminacy of design.

83
00:07:16,264 --> 00:07:19,324
Within the same technical constraints, there are often several feasible options.

84
00:07:19,324 --> 00:07:22,134
But “relative” means that not every design is possible.

85
00:07:23,114 --> 00:07:26,049
The second point is the technical code, or “technical code.”

86
00:07:26,049 --> 00:07:27,778
This is not software source code.

87
00:07:27,778 --> 00:07:33,117
The technical code is how social relations, interests, and a cultural horizon settle into design parameters.

88
00:07:33,117 --> 00:07:37,864
Once stabilized, the results of social choices and conflicts appear as if they were “technical necessity.”

89
00:07:38,844 --> 00:07:41,062
The third point is users’ capacity to act.

90
00:07:41,062 --> 00:07:46,670
Feenberg gives an example: email on the Internet was introduced by skilled users, although it was not in the designers’ original plan;

91
00:07:46,670 --> 00:07:52,512
yet in a 2003 lecture, he observed that it had become the Internet’s most widely used function.

92
00:07:52,512 --> 00:07:56,069
This capacity is real but conditional; it is not unlimited control.

93
00:07:57,049 --> 00:08:04,403
The fourth point is democratic rationalization: expanding technical rationality to account for contexts, costs, and experiences previously excluded, through initiative,

94
00:08:04,403 --> 00:08:08,620
resistance, and participation by those affected, which can lead to changes in practice or design.

95
00:08:08,620 --> 00:08:13,081
It does not reject efficiency; it asks an additional question: efficiency for whom, and who bears the cost?

96
00:08:14,281 --> 00:08:20,998
Feenberg calls what affected people demand “participant interests”: anyone drawn into a technical system has interests arising from that very position.

97
00:08:21,328 --> 00:08:28,284
These interests enter technology through three paths: public debate about technology that forces design to change; dialogue between experts and users,

98
00:08:28,284 --> 00:08:33,454
as in participatory design; and creative appropriation, when users create new functions for existing technology.

99
00:08:34,654 --> 00:08:37,088
So how does Feenberg understand democracy in technology?

100
00:08:37,088 --> 00:08:42,577
In the texts our group uses, he does not offer a general definition of democracy; instead, he raises the problem of expanding democracy.

101
00:08:42,577 --> 00:08:47,542
Feenberg recalls Marx’s argument: democracy must expand from the political sphere into the world of labor.

102
00:08:47,542 --> 00:08:54,685
He argues that if democracy does not reach areas of life mediated by technology, its use-value will decline and participation will wither.

103
00:08:55,665 --> 00:09:00,405
Feenberg also makes clear that holding elections among devices or designs would be irrational.

104
00:09:00,405 --> 00:09:06,106
For him, democratizing technology is first of all not a matter of legal rights, but a matter of initiative and participation.

105
00:09:06,106 --> 00:09:11,705
Legal form would be empty if it did not arise from the experience and needs of people resisting a technically grounded hegemony.

106
00:09:12,685 --> 00:09:17,880
In the Vietnamese context, our group reads this point as follows: legal rules on participation are a necessary framework,

107
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:22,575
but they matter only when people can genuinely use them, and when their voices can reach design decisions.

108
00:09:27,775 --> 00:09:32,658
Let us examine a situation Feenberg himself analyzed: people with AIDS in the United States in the late 1980s.

109
00:09:32,988 --> 00:09:37,391
At the time, new medicines were mainly available to patients through controlled clinical trials.

110
00:09:37,391 --> 00:09:41,507
There were very few places, participation conditions were strict, and some participants received a placebo.

111
00:09:41,837 --> 00:09:47,680
According to Feenberg, this design treated patients as passive objects; their wish to participate in research was considered irrational.

112
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:50,785
But the patients spoke out collectively.

113
00:09:50,785 --> 00:09:55,306
They taught themselves about medicines and trial procedures, made specific demands to regulators, and demonstrated,

114
00:09:55,306 --> 00:10:01,672
including a gathering of more than one thousand people outside the U.S. Food and Drug Administration headquarters in October 1988.

115
00:10:02,002 --> 00:10:04,479
Under that pressure, the institution changed step by step.

116
00:10:04,479 --> 00:10:09,409
In 1987, a rule allowed investigational drugs to be used for treatment outside the formal trial.

117
00:10:09,409 --> 00:10:15,553
In 1992, a “parallel track” policy allowed people unable to enter trials to receive the drugs, together with an accelerated approval mechanism.

118
00:10:16,753 --> 00:10:22,964
Feenberg summarizes: because the old procedure could not secure patients’ cooperation, patients ultimately forced the trial design to change,

119
00:10:22,964 --> 00:10:25,557
adding care for the wider patient population to the scientific purpose.

120
00:10:25,557 --> 00:10:27,928
He considered this a democratic and progressive change.

121
00:10:28,258 --> 00:10:35,028
But it was not a complete victory: researchers worried that the data would be less reliable, and the debate between rapid access and scientific verification continued.

122
00:10:36,228 --> 00:10:41,926
When we apply the four questions above, we can see why this was democratization, not merely a design change.

123
00:10:41,926 --> 00:10:46,926
The change came from the experience of those affected; it passed through public debate and dialogue with experts;

124
00:10:46,926 --> 00:10:52,592
it served the interests of the larger patient population without abandoning scientific verification; and it became a rule with an accountable institution.

125
00:10:52,922 --> 00:10:55,112
That is the path from feedback to design revision.

126
00:11:00,312 --> 00:11:03,780
Our group chooses a familiar example: VNeID, Vietnam’s national identification application.

127
00:11:03,780 --> 00:11:09,925
The Law on Identification defines it as an application on a digital device for electronic identification and authentication in administrative procedures,

128
00:11:09,925 --> 00:11:12,937
public services, and other transactions in the electronic environment.

129
00:11:13,917 --> 00:11:16,810
This is an example for analyzing the governance of citizens’ data.

130
00:11:16,810 --> 00:11:22,611
Our group does not conclude that VNeID “is democratic” or “is not democratic,” and one application does not represent Vietnam’s entire democracy.

131
00:11:23,811 --> 00:11:29,897
Applying the matrix to this system, we ask: do people know what data are collected, for what purpose, and who can access them?

132
00:11:29,897 --> 00:11:33,775
Do they participate when needs are defined, or only after the system is already operating?

133
00:11:33,775 --> 00:11:37,451
When data are wrong, is there a path of appeal, and who checks the operators?

134
00:11:38,651 --> 00:11:41,288
Current law provides the basis for some answers.

135
00:11:41,288 --> 00:11:45,120
The Constitution protects the inviolability of private life and personal secrets.

136
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:53,978
The 2025 Law on Personal Data Protection provides the right to know about data processing, to consent or withdraw consent, to view and request correction, to complain, and to sue.

137
00:11:53,978 --> 00:12:01,092
At the same time, the law permits data processing without consent in certain cases, including state-management activities authorized by law,

138
00:12:01,092 --> 00:12:02,743
and then a supervision mechanism must be in place.

139
00:12:03,943 --> 00:12:11,208
Read through Feenberg, choices that appear purely technical—required data fields, authentication methods, viewing permissions, and log-retention periods—

140
00:12:11,208 --> 00:12:13,812
are all places where priorities can settle into a technical code.

141
00:12:13,812 --> 00:12:20,599
An optimized design for people with smartphones and digital skills may unintentionally marginalize older people, people with disabilities, or people without devices.

142
00:12:20,599 --> 00:12:23,337
The question is: whose experience is taken into account in the design?

143
00:12:24,537 --> 00:12:26,419
So at what stage should democracy be introduced?

144
00:12:26,419 --> 00:12:29,574
Our group’s answer: at every stage, through different mechanisms.

145
00:12:29,574 --> 00:12:32,331
Needs assessment: map the people affected.

146
00:12:32,331 --> 00:12:35,625
Design: make the purpose, data, and criteria public.

147
00:12:35,625 --> 00:12:38,020
Testing: test with groups likely to be overlooked.

148
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:41,279
Operation: provide support, correction, and complaint channels.

149
00:12:41,279 --> 00:12:43,218
Oversight: conduct independent checks.

150
00:12:43,218 --> 00:12:45,918
Redesign: feedback must be able to change the rules.

151
00:12:46,898 --> 00:12:51,325
The competent authority still makes the final decision; engineers remain responsible for professional matters.

152
00:12:51,325 --> 00:12:55,612
Democratization does not give everyone unlimited power over every detail, and it certainly does not let anyone read someone else’s data.

153
00:12:55,612 --> 00:12:59,879
It requires the voices of affected people to have a route to decisions, and those decisions must have reasons.

154
00:13:01,079 --> 00:13:05,238
People often think that effective management and protecting citizens’ rights are opposite ends of a balance.

155
00:13:05,238 --> 00:13:08,454
In reality, this is not always so: good design can improve both.

156
00:13:08,784 --> 00:13:16,129
The law requires data to be collected only within a specific, clear purpose and scope: collecting less means exposing less and storing less.

157
00:13:16,129 --> 00:13:21,561
Access logs help people know who viewed their records and help managers detect unauthorized searches.

158
00:13:21,561 --> 00:13:27,763
A simple channel for correcting wrong data protects rights and makes the database more accurate, because people often detect errors first.

159
00:13:28,093 --> 00:13:33,121
When a real conflict exists, the Constitution allows rights to be restricted only by law and only when truly necessary.

160
00:13:38,321 --> 00:13:44,155
Four concepts are often mixed up: privacy is a human right; personal-data protection is a rule for processing data;

161
00:13:44,155 --> 00:13:48,360
information security is a measure against unauthorized access; access control decides who may see what.

162
00:13:48,360 --> 00:13:50,887
A very secure system can still grant access too broadly.

163
00:13:52,087 --> 00:13:54,562
Now consider an illustrative model created by our group.

164
00:13:54,562 --> 00:13:57,260
Option A: one “submit feedback” box.

165
00:13:57,260 --> 00:13:59,626
People submit their feedback, then do not know where it goes.

166
00:13:59,956 --> 00:14:08,228
Option B: the same box, but with published processing criteria, an identity-protecting tracking code, a reasoned response, an anonymized summary,

167
00:14:08,228 --> 00:14:11,906
a channel to request reconsideration, and a log of changes made after feedback.

168
00:14:12,236 --> 00:14:14,225
Both options allow “feedback.”

169
00:14:14,225 --> 00:14:17,301
But only Option B creates a route for an opinion to reach a decision.

170
00:14:18,501 --> 00:14:23,293
Moderation is not always bad: filtering spam or blocking content that violates others’ rights is necessary.

171
00:14:23,293 --> 00:14:28,561
The question is whether the filtering criteria are public, and whether filtered users can know why and request reconsideration.

172
00:14:28,561 --> 00:14:31,377
Without these conditions, “being allowed to give feedback” is only formal participation.

173
00:14:36,577 --> 00:14:43,944
A common question is whether registering a patent or protecting copyright, including for products made with artificial intelligence, is democratizing technology.

174
00:14:45,144 --> 00:14:50,956
Under Vietnam’s Intellectual Property Law, copyright arises when a work is created and expressed in a material form,

175
00:14:50,956 --> 00:14:56,984
regardless of whether it has been registered; rights in an invention are established on the basis of a protection certificate issued by a state agency.

176
00:14:56,984 --> 00:14:59,508
This is a legal mechanism for protecting rights and resolving disputes.

177
00:15:00,488 --> 00:15:07,293
In one sense, this is an expression of democracy: the law protects creators’ equal rights, and preventing copyright infringement protects those rights.

178
00:15:07,623 --> 00:15:15,199
But to contribute to the democratization of technology, more is needed: procedures must be accessible to people with fewer resources, and knowledge must not be concentrated in a small number of actors.

179
00:15:15,199 --> 00:15:19,988
Protecting ownership is the foundation; affected people’s participation in design decisions is the next step.

180
00:15:20,968 --> 00:15:28,765
For artificial intelligence, the 2025 amendment assigns the Government to provide specific rules for products using AI; our group makes no general conclusion for every case.

181
00:15:33,965 --> 00:15:37,017
This is Group 14’s argument, not a quotation from Feenberg.

182
00:15:37,347 --> 00:15:41,204
Democratizing technology does not stop at expanding access or allowing people to submit opinions.

183
00:15:41,204 --> 00:15:48,588
It requires affected people to have meaningful channels to participate in defining the problem, design criteria, data mechanisms, power controls, privacy,

184
00:15:48,588 --> 00:15:54,820
oversight, and appeals; and that participation must produce verifiable feedback within technical, legal, and safety limits.

185
00:15:56,020 --> 00:16:02,653
Therefore, we should: identify affected people before designing; disclose purpose, data, and responsibility without violating privacy;

186
00:16:02,653 --> 00:16:09,233
consult early and respond with reasons; apply least-privilege access, access logs, and an appeal path; and assess impacts before and after deployment.

187
00:16:10,213 --> 00:16:18,174
We should not: equate downloading an application with democratization; offer symbolic feedback; close oversight channels in the name of efficiency; publish personal data in the name of transparency;

188
00:16:18,174 --> 00:16:19,819
or replace technical verification with a vote.

189
00:16:21,019 --> 00:16:22,359
So can it be done?

190
00:16:22,359 --> 00:16:23,742
Yes, but it is not easy.

191
00:16:24,072 --> 00:16:26,849
The first obstacle is the cost and time required for consultation.

192
00:16:26,849 --> 00:16:31,358
The second is the digital divide: older people, and people lacking devices or skills, can easily be left behind.

193
00:16:31,358 --> 00:16:35,949
The third is system safety: the more open the participation channel, the more carefully data must be protected.

194
00:16:35,949 --> 00:16:40,864
The fourth is the information imbalance between experts and citizens, together with legal limits on secrecy and security.

195
00:16:41,194 --> 00:16:45,284
Therefore, democratization must proceed step by step, with a roadmap and accountable people.

196
00:16:46,484 --> 00:16:51,651
The solution must answer three questions: who supervises, who can request changes, and who is responsible for remediation.

197
00:16:51,981 --> 00:16:54,880
For a system such as VNeID, current law already provides answers.

198
00:16:54,880 --> 00:17:02,578
Oversight: the National Assembly, People’s Councils, the Vietnam Fatherland Front, the specialized personal-data protection authority, and, at commune level, the People’s Inspection Board.

199
00:17:02,578 --> 00:17:10,547
Requests for change: citizens themselves, through the right to request correction of identification information under the Law on Identification, the right to request correction of personal data, complaints, and petitions.

200
00:17:10,547 --> 00:17:16,796
Remediation: the identification-management authority and the data controller must correct errors; the complaint resolver must respond within the statutory time limit;

201
00:17:16,796 --> 00:17:20,710
and when unlawful conduct causes damage, the State has a compensation mechanism.

202
00:17:21,910 --> 00:17:29,203
On that basis, our group proposes: periodic independent technical audits with published summaries; a public change log for each version;

203
00:17:29,203 --> 00:17:36,809
a feature allowing people to know who viewed their data; and indicators such as the rate of reasoned responses, appeal-processing time, and the number of design changes made after feedback.

204
00:17:37,139 --> 00:17:42,658
These proposals cannot remove every conflict, but they make conflict visible, open to debate, and assigned to someone accountable.

205
00:17:47,858 --> 00:17:51,144
Let us return to the opening question: where does democracy lie in a technological system?

206
00:17:51,694 --> 00:17:54,675
It does not lie in merely being able to use it, nor only in a feedback box.

207
00:17:54,675 --> 00:18:01,590
It lies in affected people being able to know, receive a response to consultation, participate in decisions where appropriate, supervise, and request changes;

208
00:18:01,590 --> 00:18:04,005
and in someone being responsible for remediation when the system is wrong.

209
00:18:05,205 --> 00:18:08,468
Within Vietnam, these democratic values already have a foundation.

210
00:18:08,468 --> 00:18:16,155
The Constitution affirms that state power belongs to the People and that citizens may participate in management and receive responses to petitions; the principle that the people know, discuss, act, inspect,

211
00:18:16,155 --> 00:18:19,185
supervise, and benefit provides direction.

212
00:18:19,515 --> 00:18:24,031
The challenge is to bring these principles into the design and governance of the digital systems people use every day.

213
00:18:25,231 --> 00:18:31,701
Methodologically, Feenberg helps us avoid two mistakes: treating technology as a neutral tool, and treating technology as destiny.

214
00:18:31,701 --> 00:18:34,621
Technology carries values, but it can be guided.

215
00:18:34,621 --> 00:18:37,712
And who gets to participate in guiding it is itself a democratic question.

216
00:18:38,912 --> 00:18:43,120
Group 14 sincerely thanks lecturer Nguyễn Hữu Sơn for guiding our group throughout the course.

217
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:44,451
Thank you all for watching.
